                %a{:href => "#triangulable_spaces"} Triangulable spaces
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#examples_from_manifold_theory"} Examples from manifold theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_simplicial_sets"} Relation to simplicial sets
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea_and_definition Idea and definition
          %p
            A polyhedron (beware remark
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RemarkOnTerminology"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            made up of very simple bits ‘glued’ together. The ‘bits’ are
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplices"} simplices
            of different
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensions"} dimensions
            %em simplicial complex
            is a neat combinatorial way of giving the corresponding ‘gluing’ instructions, a bit like the plan of a construction kit!
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong simplicial complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              (sometimes called an
              %strong abstract
              simplicial complex) consists of
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                  of objects,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V(K)
                  %strong vertices
              %li
                %p
                  a set,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(K)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V(K)
                  = succeed "." do
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplices
            %p such that the simplices satisfy the following conditions:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \subset V(K)
                  is a simplex and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi τ
                          %mo ⊂
                          %mi σ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau \subset \sigma
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi τ
                          %mo ≠
                          %mi ∅
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau \ne \emptyset
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi τ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
                  is also a simplex;
              %li
                %p
                  every singleton
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi v
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{v\}
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi v
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in V(K)
          %p
            We say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
            is a
            %strong face
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in S(K)
            has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p+1
            elements it is said to be a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                \-simplex
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is denoted by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_p
            %strong dimension
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is the largest
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_p
            is non-empty.
          %p
            A
            %strong map
            of simplicial complexes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \to L
            is a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: V(K) \to V(L)
            such that whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \subseteq V(K)
            belongs to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(K)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(\sigma)
            belongs to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(L)
          #RemarkOnTerminology.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The word ‘polyhedron’ is used here as it is often used by
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topologists
              = succeed "." do
                %em Proofs and Refutations
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Recall that a ‘simple’ graph is an undirected
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
                with no loops or multiple edges. A simple graph is essentially the same thing as a 1-dimensional simplicial complex, by interpreting edges as simplices and vice versa (see
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph#undirected_graphs_as_1complexes_barycentric_subdivision"} undirected graphs as 1-complexes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
                is essentially the same thing as a ‘directed multigraph’, i.e., a
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            %li
              %p
                Given a space and an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+methods"} Čech methods
                and the discussion there). The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vietoris+complex"} Vietoris complex
                is another given by a related method.
            %li
              %p
                Given any two sets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\subseteq X\times Y
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dowker%27s+theorem"} Dowker's theorem
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,\leq)
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
                of the associated category has a simple description. The vertices are the points of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                and the simplices are the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flag"} flags
            %li
              %p
                %strong Buildings:
                An important class of simplicial complexes is provided by the notion of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/building"} building
          %h2#vsSSet Simplicial complexes v. simplicial sets
          %p
            Simplicial complexes are, in some sense, special cases of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial sets
          %p
            To get from a simplicial complex to a fairly small simplicial set, you pick a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+order"} total order
            on the set of vertices. Without an order on the vertices, you cannot speak of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi k
                    %mi th
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k^{th}
            face of a simplex, which is an essential feature of a simplicial set! The degeneracies are obtained by repeating an element when listing the vertices of a simplex. If
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma = \{v_0,v_1,\ldots, v_n\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi …
                  %mo &lt;
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_0\lt v_1\lt \ldots \lt v_n
            then, for instance,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_0(\sigma) = \{v_0,v_0, v_1,\ldots, v_n\}
          %p
            If you do not want to pick an order then you can still form a simplicial set where to each
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)!
            simplices of that associated simplicial set. The result is more unwieldy, but can be useful under some circumstances as it defines a functor from the category of simplicial complexes to that of simplicial sets. This is very important when discussing group actions on simplicial complexes and how this transfers to the associated simplicial set.
          %h2#simplicial_complexes_as_sheaves_on_a_site Simplicial complexes as sheaves on a site
          %p
            Simplicial sets are essentially (that is, up to equivalence)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            of finite nonempty totally ordered sets, whereas simplicial complexes may be regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+sheaf"} concrete presheaves
            on the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Fin
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fin_{+}
            of finite nonempty sets and functions between them. This works as follows: given a simplicial complex,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K = (V(K), S(K))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Fin
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^\sim: Fin_{+}^{op} \to Set
            whose values are sets of functions
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo =
                    %mi def
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo =
                    %mi def
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^\sim(B) \stackrel{def}{=} \{\phi: B \to V(K)| \phi(B) \in S(K)\}, \qquad K^\sim(f: A \to B)(\phi) \stackrel{def}{=} \phi \circ f
          %p
            This defines an evident
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SimpComplex
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Fin
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mi op
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SimpComplex \to Set^{Fin_{+}^{op}}: K \mapsto K^\sim
          %p
            that is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Fin
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon Fin_{+}^{op} \to Set
            is
            %strong concrete
            if the canonical map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(B) \to F(1)^{\hom(1, B)}
          %p
            is an injection. The point is that a morphism of concrete presheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \to G
            is uniquely determined from the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1) \to G(1)
            between their underlying sets (i.e., the underlying-set functor on concrete presheaves is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} structure
            – that’s what makes it “concrete”).
          %p
            (Equivalently but somewhat more elaborately, the category of concrete presheaves is the same as the full subcategory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+sheaf"} concrete sheaves
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Fin
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fin_{+}
            with respect to the trivial
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \hookrightarrow hom(-, D)
            is the maximal sieve.)
          %p
            From this point of view, it is immediate that simplicial complexes are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+presheaf"} separated objects
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+topology"} Lawvere-Tierney topology
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Fin
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{Fin_{+}^{op}}
            whose sheaves are sets, via the sheafification functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo =
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ev
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Fin
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma = \hom(\mathbf{1}, -) = ev_1 \colon Set^{Fin_{+}^{op}} \to Set
          %p
            which has a right adjoint. (See
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+topos"} local topos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitopos"} quasitopos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %h2#geometric_realisations_and_polyhedra Geometric realisations and Polyhedra
          %p An abstract simplicial complex is a combinatorial gadget that models certain aspects of a spatial configuration. Sometimes it is useful, perhaps even necessary, to produce a topological space from that data in a simplicial complex.
          %h3#idea Idea
          %p
            To each simplicial complex
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %em polyhedron
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            often also called the
            %em geometric realisation
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            and denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|
          %p
            This can be constructed by taking a copy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\sigma)
            of a standard topological
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            and then ‘gluing’ them together according to the face relations encoded in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %p
            We therefore first need the definition of a standard
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              The
              %em
                standard (topological)
                = succeed "-simplex" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is (usually) taken to be the convex hull of the basis vectors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi e
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi e
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{e}_1, \mathbf{e}_2,\ldots, \mathbf{e}_{p+1}
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mi p
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{p+1}
          %h3#intuition Intuition
          %p
            The geometric realisation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|
            of a simplicial complex,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is then constructed by taking, for each abstract
            = succeed "-simplex," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma\in S(K)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\sigma)
            of such a standard topological
            = succeed "-simplex," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
            is a face of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            we identify
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi τ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\tau)
            with the corresponding face of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\sigma)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^p
          %h3#canonical_construction Canonical construction
          %p
            As a set,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|
            is constructed as follows:
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|
            is the set of all functions from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V(K)
            to the closed interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            such that
          %ul
            %li
              if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo ∈
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in {|K|}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{v \in V(K) \mid \alpha(v)  \neq  0\}
          %p
            is a simplex of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %ul
            %li
              for each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo ∈
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in {|K|}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi v
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{v \in V(K)} \alpha (v) = 1.
          %p
            There are two commonly used topologies on the set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|
            = succeed ":" do
              %em metric topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} )
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(\alpha,\beta) = \Big(\sum_{v\in V(K)} (\alpha(v) - \beta(v))^2\Big)^\frac{1}{2}.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|
            , when endowed with the metric space topology, will be denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi K
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|_d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V(K)
            is finite, this gives
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi K
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|_d
            as a subspace of the metric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mo #
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{\#(V(K))}
            (which is usually of much higher dimension than might seem geometrically significant in a given context).
          %p
            The second topology is the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em coherent topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |s|
            consists of all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo ∈
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in {|K|}
            supported in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi K
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|_d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|
            is the largest topology for which all inclusions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ↪
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|s|} \hookrightarrow {|K|}
            are continuous. This topological space is normally denoted just
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|
          %p
            Note that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \subseteq t
            is an inclusion of simplices in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|s|} \hookrightarrow {|t|}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |K|
            may then be characterized as the colimit in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            of the diagram consisting of geometric simplices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |s|
            and inclusions between them, so that a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: {|K|} \to X
            is continuous if and only if its restriction to each simplex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |s|
            is continuous. In particular, the identity function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|K|} \to {|K|}_d
            is continuous, so that the coherent topology contains the metric topology (and is often strictly larger).
          %ul
            %li
              %strong Warning:
              The geometric realization of a simplicial complex does not preserve products. Indeed, the product of two intervals in the category of simplicial complexes is the tetrahedron!
          %h2#triangulable_spaces Triangulable spaces
          %p
            If a topological space can be described up to homeomorphism as the geometric realization of a simplicial complex, we say it is
            = succeed "," do
              %strong triangulable
            %strong triangulation
            of a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a simplicial complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            together with a homeomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: |K| \to X
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+triangulation"} classical triangulation
          %p
            There is another stronger notion of triangulable used by geometric topologists: a
            %strong piecewise-linear (PL) structure
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+manifold"} topological manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is given by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} PL atlas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to V
            is
            %strong piecewise linear
            if its graph is the intersection of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \times V
            with a semilinear set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is given by a finite Boolean combination of solution sets of linear inequalities.) A triangulation is then called compatible, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: |K| \to X
            is piecewise linear. Every PL manifold admits a compatible triangulation.
          %h4#examples_from_manifold_theory Examples from manifold theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                All
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} smooth manifolds
                are triangulable and, in fact, admit canonical PL structures.
            %li
              %p All topological manifolds in dimensions 2 and 3 admit PL structures, and are in fact smoothable (admit a smooth manifold structure).
            %li
              %p
                The
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mn 8
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_8
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E8+manifold"} manifold
                does not admit a triangulation, much less a PL structure.
            %li
              %p
                In dimensions
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 5
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 5
                = succeed "-fold" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-2)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+sphere"} Poincaré sphere
                is homeomorphic to the
                = succeed "-sphere." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "-sphere." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p
                The manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mn 8
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_8 \times T^k
                is triangulable for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \geq 1
                but does not admit a PL structure.
          %h4#relation_to_simplicial_sets Relation to simplicial sets
          %p The following statement may seem obvious, but it requires careful proof:
          %ul
            %li A space is triangulable if and only if it is homeomorphic to the geometric realization of a simplicial set.
          %p As an important step:
          %ul
            %li The geometric realization of the nerve of a poset is triangulable.
          %p
            The basic technique is to use
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subdivision"} subdivision
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulation"}> triangulation
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulation+theorem"} triangulation theorem
          %h2#references References
          %p Textbook account:
          %ul
            %li#Spanier66
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edwin+Spanier"}> Edwin Spanier
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algebraic topology
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4684-9322-1"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4684-9322-1
          %p Exposition:
          %ul
            %li#Erleben2010
              Kenny Erleben,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Simplicial complexes
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Erleben_SimplicialComplexes.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            On simplicial complexes forming a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitopos"} quasitopos
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+sheaves"} concrete sheaves
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Hoffnung"} Alex Hoffnung
              = succeed "," do
                %em Convenient Categories of Smooth Spaces
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0807.1704"} (arXiv)
          %p
            Mechanical computation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
            of simplicial complexes:
          %ul
            %li
              Georg Frenck:
              %em SiDra
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://github.com/georgfrenck/SiDra/"}> github
              , demo:
              %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JBbOWaeZRk4"}> YT
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            See also the program
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenzo"} Kenzo
